WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:12.199
So the other kind of part of this puzzle in terms of whether or not we're considering things for whether we should be replicating data to an actor is dormancy.

2
00:00:12.199 --> 00:00:23.199
Dormancy essentially is the concept of whether when relevancy is kind of okay, this thing is kind of like probably should be considered,

3
00:00:23.199 --> 00:00:35.200
but are there kind of extra factors that we can kind of bear in mind that maybe would mean that, you know, it's too far away or it's on the wrong team or I'm the wrong owner and therefore it's not relevant.

4
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:43.259
Dormancy is a bit more of a kind of global idea of, you know, actually no one needs to care about this thing at all right now.

5
00:00:43.259 --> 00:00:48.259
So it's kind of it's a bit more of a global state. It's less of a connection by connection basis.

6
00:00:48.259 --> 00:01:05.260
And so taking, for example, we've got like in gameplay code, we have we've done some kind of game set up and we have a cube that has a color variable and that color variable is replicated, right?

7
00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:14.260
So by what we've kind of seen, we've we talked before about the idea that we can do like a net cull, a net frequency update.

8
00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:21.260
This is this value about how frequently this value updates. So say, I don't know, it's every 100 milliseconds.

9
00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:29.260
We can still imagine that our we've talked before about the fact that the server is having to kind of like check its shadow set of data, seeing if it's the same as the client.

10
00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:34.260
So even if we reduce our net relevancy to like, you know, 100 milliseconds every 0.1 seconds,

11
00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:43.260
that's still going to be checking against this shadow set of data 10 times a second for whether or not this cube has the right color against every other client.

12
00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:57.260
That's 10 times more checks than we know is happening, right? Obviously, in this particular situation, you could align your net update with the kind of frequency with which we're changing this color.

13
00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:05.260
But if you imagine some kind of more complex scenario or if those two things aren't perfectly in sync, suddenly it becomes not a perfect tool to solve that problem.

14
00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:14.419
If we just have some kind of, if you could think about some other kind of like game event, like something like a door opening, for example,

15
00:02:14.419 --> 00:02:20.419
the, you know, we don't need every player to be constantly checking, hey, is this door open?

16
00:02:20.419 --> 00:02:26.419
We know that that kind of that door will never be open until someone interacts with it, and then it becomes open.

17
00:02:26.419 --> 00:02:33.419
And it's only at the point that it opens that information changes. And at the point that it's open, again, it will stay open until someone else interacts with it.

18
00:02:33.419 --> 00:02:38.419
So we don't really need our server to be constantly checking against this information.

19
00:02:38.419 --> 00:02:41.419
That's where dormancy can kind of help us.

20
00:02:42.419 --> 00:02:52.419
Dormancy basically gives us the opportunity to say, you know, for a start, this thing will perhaps, you know, never become relevant,

21
00:02:52.419 --> 00:02:59.419
but also that this object can be like irrelevant to every connection until some particular event happens.

22
00:02:59.419 --> 00:03:10.419
So if we were to take our cube changing color every second, what we could do is we could set that cube to be net dormant.

23
00:03:10.419 --> 00:03:15.419
So dormant all is kind of one setting that you could choose, but we can also set it to be dormant initial.

24
00:03:15.419 --> 00:03:23.419
And that would essentially say, just presume server that I am dormant right now.

25
00:03:23.419 --> 00:03:30.419
And then the way you can temporarily break that, so this is what we'll kind of go into this flow in a little bit.

26
00:03:30.419 --> 00:03:38.419
But when something is dormant initial, what it essentially means is I'm not even going to bother checking any of the other stuff about this thing.

27
00:03:38.419 --> 00:03:40.419
I'm just going to ignore this thing.

28
00:03:40.419 --> 00:03:50.419
And then the way that the kind of other side of that coin to setting something as dormant initial is use this function called set net dormancy or flush net dormancy.

29
00:03:50.419 --> 00:04:01.780
And that's essentially notifying the server, hey, some relevant bit of information about this thing has changed and therefore make this thing temporarily undormant.

30
00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:11.780
And now you can bother doing the rest of the checks about like relevancy to see if it's within distance, to see if it's, you know, on the same team, if it's got the right connection.

31
00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:16.779
But that way you've kind of saved the server all of those checks in the first place.

32
00:04:16.779 --> 00:04:20.779
And we know for a fact that we only change this cube's color on a certain frequency.

33
00:04:20.779 --> 00:04:25.779
So it's only when we change that cube's color that we would flush its net dormancy.

34
00:04:26.779 --> 00:04:34.259
So by default, when you're looking at an actor, if you go to dormancy, by default, it's awake.

35
00:04:34.259 --> 00:04:38.259
That means that basically this thing is assumed to be kind of dormant.

36
00:04:38.259 --> 00:04:43.259
Hypothetically, it could go undormant. Game code could affect that.

37
00:04:43.259 --> 00:04:48.259
But by default, it's just going to be like, I'm kind of assuming that I'm mostly undormant all the time.

38
00:04:48.259 --> 00:04:57.259
But the other kind of states that you can be aware of, you've got never, which is a little bit like our kind of always relevant is to basically say this thing will never go dormant.

39
00:04:57.259 --> 00:05:01.259
You know, it's always going to be out of dormancy.

40
00:05:01.259 --> 00:05:07.259
But the kind of more save your CPU cycles, save your connection is to be an initial.

41
00:05:07.259 --> 00:05:14.259
And this would say that from the beginning of the game, this thing is kind of irrelevant until I say otherwise.

42
00:05:14.259 --> 00:05:18.259
And then you would do this kind of, you know, flush net dormancy.

43
00:05:18.259 --> 00:05:20.259
And that's the kind of, like I said, the other side of this coin.

44
00:05:20.259 --> 00:05:30.259
This would temporarily set this thing to not be dormant anymore for like until every client has kind of received that whatever updated state.

45
00:05:30.259 --> 00:05:32.259
And then it would go back to being dormant.

46
00:05:32.259 --> 00:05:34.259
So that's kind of the other side of this coin.

47
00:05:34.259 --> 00:05:42.949
The other thing that you can use is, if I go back to my defaults, is dormant all.

48
00:05:42.949 --> 00:05:50.949
And that essentially means that this thing might currently not be dormant, but kind of wants to become dormant as soon as it can.

49
00:05:50.949 --> 00:05:56.949
So that might be for an actor that initially might need its state replicated to act as on first connection.

50
00:05:56.949 --> 00:05:59.949
But once that's done, then it can kind of go to being dormant.

51
00:05:59.949 --> 00:06:07.949
So dormant initially is be like, assume that whatever state I've been put in in the world now is just my starting state.

52
00:06:07.949 --> 00:06:11.949
I'm kind of baked into the level and no one needs to care about me until I say so.

53
00:06:11.949 --> 00:06:16.949
This is similar, but it's just to say, you know, right at the beginning, I might have some state that I need to replicate,

54
00:06:16.949 --> 00:06:23.949
but almost immediately I kind of want to become dormant again so the game will naturally boil everything down.
